\f2\b \cf4 \strokec4 What you are seeing:
\f1\b0 \
Le L\'e9zard aux plumes d'or - The Lizard with Golden Feathers - 1971. An illustrated book with forty color lithographs including wrapper and cover, on publisher's vellum. Edition of 150. Publisher Louis Broder, Paris. Lithographs printed by Mourlot Fr\'e8res and Fequet et Baudier, Paris. Gift of Boris Fridman, Newton, MA, to MFA in 2021.\

\f2\b The story for visitors:
\f1\b0 \
Joan Mir\'f3 is known as a painter. He was also a writer. In 1948 he began writing a surrealist poem - Le L\'e9zard aux plumes d'or - about a lizard, feathers, metamorphosis. He decided the text could only be published if he controlled every part: words, images, paper, printing.\
He took it to\'a0
\f2\b Louis Broder
\f1\b0 . Broder was a publisher in Paris who specialized in livres d'artiste - limited edition books where the publisher finances the project and brings artist, poet, and printers together on the same press. Broder had been working with the same printers since after the war -\'a0
\f2\b F\'e9quet et Baudier
\f1\b0 \'a0for text and\'a0
\f2\b Mourlot Fr\'e8res
\f1\b0 \'a0for lithography.\
Mourlot Fr\'e8res was founded in 1852 on Rue de Chabrol. Under Fernand Mourlot from 1930, it became the place where artists drew directly on limestone. Vlaminck and Utrillo first, then Picasso, Matisse, Chagall, Mir\'f3. From 1948 on, Mir\'f3 worked with Mourlot, who also instructed Picasso and Chagall.\
For Le L\'e9zard, Mir\'f3 and his printers at atelier Mourlot printed most of the edition in 1967. Then they stopped. Mir\'f3 decided the paper was flawed and would not hold his saturated color. They pulped the edition and began again, finally issuing it in 1971 on publisher's vellum - chosen because it holds color and is durable.\
That is why this book is important for Picasso, Mir\'f3, Dal\'ed: Unbound:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf4 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 It shows the method that links all three artists in this exhibition - Broder's model where publisher requires artist + poet + printer to collaborate.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 It shows why Mourlot matters - without Mourlot Fr\'e8res, there is no lithographic book by Mir\'f3, Picasso, or Dal\'ed. All three used the same workshop.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb5 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 It shows what Unbound means - Mir\'f3 was unbound from canvas. He wrote the poem, made 40 lithographs, chose vellum, destroyed a whole edition over paper quality, and donated example now here.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb5 This copy was collected by Boris Fridman and gifted to MFA Boston, which is why you see it in the Lois and Michael Torf Gallery today.\

\f2\b One sentence to close Stop 1 and lead to Stop 2:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 Mir\'f3 wrote his own text and destroyed his own book to get it right; Dal\'ed in the next room did the opposite - he took someone else's text, Freud's, and turned it into an art object on lambskin.\
