\fs32 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec6 When guiding visitors through the 
\f4\i \strokec6 Picasso, Mir\'f3, Dal\'ed: Unbound
\f0\i0 \strokec6  exhibition at the \ul \strokec6 Museum of Fine Arts, Boston\ulnone \strokec6 , 
\f5\b "Le L\'e9zard aux plumes d\'92or"
\f0\b0  (
\f4\i \strokec6 The Lizard with Golden Feathers
\f0\i0 \strokec6 ) provides a great story of how a 
\f4\i \strokec6 livre d'artiste
\f0\i0 \strokec6  can completely merge text, art, and the physical act of printmaking into a singular vision.\
The story can be broken down for museum visitors through these talking points:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \strokec6 \

\f5\b\fs40 The Artist as the Author\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs32 \cf0 \strokec6 Unlike traditional artist books where an illustrator creates images to match someone else's poetry, 
\f5\b Joan Mir\'f3 wrote the surrealist poem himself
\f0\b0 . He chose not to have his words set in standard typography. Instead, he wrote out the text by hand directly onto the lithographic stones. This transformed his handwriting into a graphic element, blurring the line between reading a poem and looking at a drawing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \strokec6 \

\f5\b\fs40 The Master Publisher's Matchmaking\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs32 \cf0 \strokec6 The visionary French publisher 
\f5\b Louis Broder
\f0\b0  acted as the catalyst for the project. Broder was famous for pairing avant-garde artists with luxury mediums. In the mid-1950s, he formed a tight-knit partnership with Mir\'f3 to produce a curated line of highly experimental graphic portfolios. Broder gave Mir\'f3 complete freedom and the financial backing to spend years perfecting 
\f4\i \strokec6 Le L\'e9zard
\f0\i0 \strokec6 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 \strokec6 \

\f5\b\fs40 The Technical Triumph at Mourlot Fr\'e8res\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs32 \cf0 \strokec6 To realize this vision, Broder and Mir\'f3 partnered with 
\f5\b Mourlot Fr\'e8res
\f0\b0 , the premier 20th-century lithography studio in Paris.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f5\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec6 The Long Delay:
\f0\b0  Mir\'f3 initially drafted the portfolio to be completed around 1967. However, the printmaking process was so technically demanding and collaborative that it took 
\f5\b four years of back-and-forth experimentation
\f0\b0  before it was finally published in 1971.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f5\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec6 The Visual Language:
\f0\b0  The team at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mourloteditions.com/products/les-lezards-aux-plumes-by-joan-miro?srsltid=AfmBOoqIZtuqJUGO6K6RhODR56DRnznGAaGHVKI3TdLZgsIDhCb-Cimc"}}{\fldrslt \cf7 \ul \ulc7 \strokec7 Mourlot Studio}} pulled off a monumental feat by printing 
\f5\b 40 vibrant color lithographs
\f0\b0  that seamlessly weave around Mir\'f3\'92s handwritten text.\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f5\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec6 The Somber Mood:
\f0\b0  Visitors looking closely at the prints will notice a heavy dominance of thick black lines contrasting against the bright splashes of color. This heavy black ink reflects the darker, tragic mood holding sway over Mir\'f3\'92s psyche during the late 1960s.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec6 Ultimately, the copy on display at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mfa.org/exhibition/picasso-miro-dali-unbound"}}{\fldrslt \cf7 \ul \ulc7 \strokec7 Museum of Fine Arts, Boston}}\'97bound in its original luxury publisher's vellum\'97stands as a monument to what happens when an artist has the perfect publisher and the world's best printmakers to back his wildest concepts.\
